<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Program.cs declares no namespace (top-level statements), and `public partial class Program`
         at the bottom is what WebApplicationFactory<Program> binds to. Keeping the root namespace
         at po_prostu_silka preserves both. -->
    <RootNamespace>po_prostu_silka</RootNamespace>
    <!--
      PINNED, and load-bearing twice. Do not change it to match the project file name.

      1. Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing emits a WebApplicationFactoryContentRootAttribute into the
         TEST assembly keyed by the entry-point assembly name. Holding the name constant keeps that
         key stable across the split, so a stale test obj/ cannot yield the confusing
         "could not find a part of the path ...\src".
      2. `dotnet publish` produces po-prostu-silka.dll, which is what App Service starts. Holding the
         name constant means the deploy artifact is byte-for-byte the same shape as before the split.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>po-prostu-silka</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      AD0001 is "an analyzer threw an exception", and exactly one analyzer throws here:
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Analyzers.RouteHandlers.RouteHandlerAnalyzer, with
      System.IndexOutOfRangeException, on ASP.NET Core 10.0.11 / SDK 10.0.400. Suppressed
      2026-09-19 (S-18 phase 7).

      TRIGGER, established by isolating it rather than guessing: a route handler passed as a method
      group whose method lives in a REFERENCED assembly. Binding a method group from this same
      assembly does not throw at all. Measured: 120 warnings for the 60 Map* calls (two per call);
      adding one more cross-assembly binding took it to 122, adding a same-assembly one changed
      nothing.

      That shape is deliberate and is what S-18 exists to produce - the handler bodies live in
      Application and only route registration lives here - so the code is not going to change to
      placate a crashing analyzer. Method groups are a supported route-handler form (see the
      RDG002 docs), so this is a tooling bug, not a pattern warning.

      COST OF THIS SUPPRESSION: RouteHandlerAnalyzer's real diagnostics are lost for this project,
      and so would be any OTHER analyzer crash here. The routes themselves are covered by
      EndpointAuthorizationTests plus the per-suite endpoint tests.

      ON THE NEXT SDK OR ASP.NET CORE UPGRADE: delete this line, build, and see whether it is still
      needed. If the build is clean, leave it deleted.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);AD0001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- BOTH are needed, not just Infrastructure. The 13 Map*Endpoints() classes now live here in
         Api/Endpoints/, but every route they register binds a handler in Application by method
         group, so the Application reference is what makes those ~60 types resolvable. Independently,
         the Testing-only probes at the bottom of Program.cs use AuthorizationPolicies from
         Infrastructure. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Application\po-prostu-silka.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Infrastructure\po-prostu-silka.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Monitor.OpenTelemetry.AspNetCore" Version="1.6.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!--
      The SECOND copy of the design-time tooling, and it is not redundant: Infrastructure declares it
      with PrivateAssets="all", which does not flow transitively. Api is the `dotnet ef` startup
      project, so without its own reference every migration command fails with "your startup project
      doesn't reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design".
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.11" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NO <Compile Remove="app\**" /> block. The old single project sat at src/ and therefore contained
    src/app/ (the Angular workspace), which had to be excluded by hand. This project's directory is
    src/Api/, so src/app/ is a sibling of the four projects and belongs to none of them.

    NO <Using /> items either: the Web SDK supplies them, and this is the one project that should
    have them.
  -->

</Project>
